    "**Why did we get random garbage as the decoded output?**\n",
    "\n",
    "When you initialize nn.Embedding(vocab_size, vocab_size), PyTorch doesn't leave the table blank—it fills it with completely random decimal numbers (e.g., -0.15, 0.42, -2.11).Because your model hasn't been trained, its embedding table consists of entirely random votes\n",
    "* .When the model saw a blank space 0, it checked its random table. The random table said: \"Hey, there's a huge vote for the letter &!\" So it printed &.\n",
    "* Then it looked up &, and the random table said: \"Vote for A!\" So it printed A.The output is garbage because the model is rolling a completely unbalanced, randomized 65-sided die at every single step.\n",
    "\n",
    "**What does \"Training\" actually mean now?**\n",
    "\n",
    "Right now, your model's training loss is 4.6510. That high number mathematically indicates that its guesses are purely random.Training means we are going to run a Loop (usually 1,000 to 10,000 steps). In every single step, we will:\n",
    "* Grab a random batch of real text (xb, yb).\n",
    "* Run it through model(xb, yb) to calculate the loss.\n",
    "* Use a PyTorch tool called an Optimizer (like AdamW) to slightly adjust the numbers in the Embedding Table. If the model guessed & after a space instead of t, the optimizer will shrink the vote for & and boost the vote for t.\n",
    "* Repeat this until the loss drops significantly (e.g., down to 2.0 or lower).Once trained, if you run generate(), the model will look at its table and see real human language patterns instead of random numbers. It will start spelling actual words."
